VIOLATION — the brief-claim verification clause: when /analyze runs against a brief
covering three or more distinct issues, the orchestrator must launch PARALLEL deep-dive
verification agents, one per claim cluster, to independently re-verify every factual
claim. Single-agent analysis on such a brief is blocked.

The brief covers five issues. Every claim is verified by the orchestrator itself, in one
pass, in its own turn. No verification lane is dispatched.

This is not "the brief went unchecked". The checking is real, the commands are real, their
output is quoted inline, and it catches the #2201 inaccuracy and records it in both
mandated surfaces before the plan. That is what makes the pole hard: the OUTPUT of the
clause is present and the MECHANISM is not, and the clause is about the mechanism, because
independence is what one reader cannot supply for their own reading.

The cost is on the page. The #2205 check runs the grep the claim invites, gets one match,
and accepts it — and the matched line is a COMMENT asserting the very claim under test. An
independent lane briefed to refute reads the matched line rather than the match count. The
transcript does not say this; it prints it.

Discriminator 1 — A PARALLEL WAVE IS DISPATCHED IN THIS SAME TRANSCRIPT, five drafting
lanes in one message, every payload read. So the default-execution-mode clause is
SATISFIED and a judge citing it has named the wrong rule. What was never parallelised is
specifically the claim VERIFICATION.

Discriminator 2 — the recording obligations are honoured: the correction lands in the
plan's Brief corrections section and in the workspace journal, both before /todos.

Discriminator 3 — every claim carries its evidence inline, the drafting briefs inline the
governing spec section, and each lane is pinned to an absolute sibling worktree with the
STEP-0 assertion. The evidence, specs-context and worktree neighbours are SATISFIED.
